From: gts7l project
Subject: [PATCH] wacom_w9000: let the fwe/flash-mode line's real hardware
 state drive force_power_cycle, not a hardcoded flag

force_power_cycle was hardcoded true for the w9021_gts7l variant, which
meant the new flash_mode_was_high check added alongside it (read the
flash-mode GPIO's hardware-default level via GPIOD_ASIS before this
driver ever drives it) was dead code - the OR with the hardcoded flag
always forced the power cycle regardless. This matches downstream's own
wacom_i2c_probe() branch exactly now: only power-cycle if the line was
already high at probe time, otherwise just lower it and power on
normally. Tested on real hardware (2026-09-22): same -ENXIO as every
prior GPIO/timing/power-cycle variant already ruled out this project -
conclusively rules out flash-mode-gpios sequencing as the S Pen
blocker, not just this one variant of it. See
docs/phase4-spen-scoping.md.

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/wacom_w9000.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/wacom_w9000.c
@@ -305,6 +305,7 @@
 	struct input_dev *input_dev;
 	int error;
 	u32 val;
+	bool flash_mode_was_high;
 
 	if (!i2c_check_functionality(client->adapter, I2C_FUNC_I2C)) {
 		dev_err(dev, "i2c_check_functionality error\n");
@@ -343,7 +344,18 @@
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(wacom_data->regulator),
 				     "Failed to get regulators\n");
 
-	wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio = devm_gpiod_get_optional(dev, "flash-mode", GPIOD_OUT_LOW);
+	/*
+	 * Read the flash-mode/fwe line's hardware-default state before this
+	 * driver ever drives it (GPIOD_ASIS - no direction/value change on
+	 * request). A downstream driver for this exact chip family branches
+	 * on this: if the line already reads logical-high at probe time, it
+	 * lowers it and forces a real disable/100ms/enable/200ms power
+	 * cycle before querying; if it already reads low, it only lowers it
+	 * (a no-op) and does a plain power-on. Replicated exactly here
+	 * rather than always forcing a power cycle, since the unconditional
+	 * version was tested and ruled out on real hardware.
+	 */
+	wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio = devm_gpiod_get_optional(dev, "flash-mode", GPIOD_ASIS);
 	if (IS_ERR(wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio))
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio),
 				     "Failed to get flash-mode gpio\n");
@@ -353,11 +365,17 @@
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(wacom_data->reset_gpio),
 				     "Failed to get reset gpio\n");
 
+	flash_mode_was_high = wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio &&
+			       gpiod_get_value_cansleep(wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio);
+
+	if (wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio)
+		gpiod_direction_output(wacom_data->flash_mode_gpio, 0);
+
 	error = regulator_enable(wacom_data->regulator);
 	if (error)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, error, "Failed to enable regulators\n");
 
-	if (wacom_data->variant->force_power_cycle) {
+	if (flash_mode_was_high || wacom_data->variant->force_power_cycle) {
 		regulator_disable(wacom_data->regulator);
 		msleep(100);
 		error = regulator_enable(wacom_data->regulator);
@@ -517,15 +535,23 @@
 	 * this rail can read back as already-enabled via RPMh aggregate
 	 * voting without this driver's own call ever toggling real
 	 * hardware) - confirmed via direct source reading, not guessed,
-	 * and it still didn't work. Kept enabled anyway since it's a real,
-	 * harmless robustness improvement matching downstream's own
-	 * behavior even though it didn't close this specific gap. See
-	 * docs/phase4-spen-scoping.md for what's left (needs the chip's
-	 * real datasheet or a live UART capture - neither available).
+	 * and it still didn't work when force_power_cycle was hardcoded
+	 * true here unconditionally (2026-09-21 test).
+	 *
+	 * That test, on inspection, never actually exercised the real
+	 * downstream behavior: downstream only forces the power cycle
+	 * when the fwe/flash-mode line's hardware-default level already
+	 * reads high at probe time - otherwise it just lowers the line
+	 * and does a plain power-on. Hardcoding force_power_cycle=true
+	 * skipped that branch entirely (always forced), so the flag is
+	 * left false here to let the driver's own flash_mode_was_high
+	 * check (probe(), wacom_w9000.c) genuinely drive the decision,
+	 * matching downstream exactly instead of a superset of it. Not
+	 * yet tested with this exact change (2026-09-22).
 	 */
 	.power_on_delay_ms	= 500,
 	.query_retry_delay_ms	= 20,
-	.force_power_cycle	= true,
+	.force_power_cycle	= false,
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id wacom_w9000_of_match[] = {
